# -*- coding: utf-8 -*-
from fams import *
F = 'igsusp'
page('instagram-suspended-for-spam-appeal', F, 'Instagram Suspended for Spam: How to Appeal',
 'Instagram suspended your account for spam or suspicious behavior? What usually triggers it, what to check, and how to appeal with the right details.',
 'Instagram suspended your account for spam?', 'Suspended for spam',
 'A spam flag usually comes from behavior, not content. Here is what tends to trigger it and how an appeal should read.',
 'A spam suspension means Instagram thinks the account behaves like automation: bursts of follows, copied comments, repeated links or mass DMs. Stop all of that, remove any connected tools, then appeal from the notice with a short, honest explanation of how you normally use the account.',
 [('Look for bursts', 'Many follows, likes, comments or DMs in a short stretch is what most spam flags are built on.'),
  ('Remove connected tools', 'Disconnect schedulers, growth tools and extensions before you appeal, or the flag can return.'),
  ('Appeal in plain words', 'Say how you use the account and what you changed. Skip long arguments.')],
 [S('What tends to look like spam', ul(['The same comment or DM sent to many people.', 'Posting the same link over and over, in captions, comments and messages.', 'Following and unfollowing in large batches.', 'Using a bulk tool that logs in from a server instead of your phone.']),
    'One or two of these is rarely the whole story, but together they are what the detection looks for.'),
  S('What to do before you appeal', ol(['Log out of every tool and extension that was connected to the account.', 'Write down what you were doing in the days before the notice, honestly.', 'Open the notice and use the in-app request to ask for a review.', 'Keep the screenshot of the notice and the date you submitted.']),
    'Submitting the appeal many times in a row does not help. One clear submission is better than five rushed ones.')],
 [('Can I appeal a spam suspension?', 'Yes, if the notice offers an appeal option. Use it once, with a clear explanation, instead of sending many requests.'),
  ('Will removing the tools help?', 'It matters. If the same behavior continues after an appeal, the flag is likely to return. Disconnect everything that automates the account.'),
  ('Does the account come back if the appeal is approved?', 'That is Instagram’s decision. Approval restores access, but you should keep activity calm afterwards.')],
 ['People who ran a scheduler, growth tool or bulk outreach.', 'Small businesses that message many customers by hand.', 'Anyone whose account was flagged after a hack used it to spam.'])

page('instagram-suspended-after-third-party-app', F, 'Instagram Suspended After Using a Third-Party App',
 'Instagram suspended your account after you used a scheduler, follower tracker or growth app? How these apps cause suspensions and what to do next.',
 'Instagram suspended after using a third-party app?', 'Suspended after a third-party app',
 'Scheduling tools, follower trackers and growth apps can all trigger a suspension. Here is what to do about it.',
 'Instagram suspends accounts when a third-party app acts on your behalf in a way it did not authorize, or asks for your password. Revoke the app, change your password and appeal once. Say plainly which app you used and that you have removed it.',
 [('Revoke the app', 'Remove it from your connected apps and delete it from your phone before you appeal.'),
  ('Change the password', 'Apps that asked for your login may still hold it. Change it where you can.'),
  ('Name the app in the appeal', 'Saying what you used and that it is gone is more credible than staying vague.')],
 [S('How apps lead to suspensions', 'There are two common patterns. Some apps log in with your password from servers in other countries, which looks like a takeover. Others automate likes, follows or comments, which looks like spam. Either can end in a suspension.',
    ul(['Follower trackers that ask for your password.', 'Growth or engagement apps that follow and like for you.', 'Schedulers that are not connected through the official business tools.', 'Browser extensions that read or act on your account.'])),
  S('What to do', ol(['Remove the app from connected apps, extensions and your phone.', 'Change the Instagram password if you can still log in, and the email password too.', 'Open the notice and ask for a review, naming the app you used.', 'Do not log in with any other tool while the appeal is open.']))],
 [('Is using one of these apps against the rules?', 'Apps that ask for your password or automate actions are not allowed. Official business tools that connect through the platform’s own login are different.'),
  ('Can Unbanz log in for me?', 'No. We never ask for your password. We help you prepare the appeal and the supporting details.'),
  ('Will the appeal mention the app?', 'It should. A short, honest line about what you used and what you removed is better than leaving it out.')],
 ['Creators who used a scheduler or growth tool.', 'Social media managers who gave an app access.', 'Anyone who entered their password into a follower tracker.'])

page('instagram-suspended-for-nudity-flag-appeal', F, 'Instagram Suspended for Nudity: Appeal Guide',
 'Instagram suspended your account for nudity or sexual content when your posts did not break the rules? How to check what was flagged and appeal.',
 'Instagram suspended you for nudity or sexual content?', 'Suspended for nudity flag',
 'Art, fitness, swimwear and health content get flagged often. This is how to read the notice and appeal.',
 'Instagram suspends accounts when it believes posts show nudity or sexual content, and automated checks get this wrong with art, swimwear, fitness and health posts. Find the flagged post in the notice, decide honestly whether it fits the rules, and appeal with a short explanation of what the content is.',
 [('Find the flagged post', 'The notice or Account Status usually names the post or reel. Start there.'),
  ('Be honest about it', 'If the post is close to the line, say what it is and why it is there. Do not argue the rules.'),
  ('Check older posts', 'Several flags build up. Remove or archive anything borderline before you appeal.')],
 [S('What is often flagged by mistake', ul(['Classical art, sculpture and museum photos.', 'Swimwear, beachwear and gym content.', 'Breastfeeding, medical and health education posts.', 'Illustrations and cartoons that look like real skin.']),
    'These can be restored on review. Content that is plainly sexual will not be.'),
  S('How to appeal', ol(['Open the notice and select the option to request a review.', 'Explain in one or two sentences what the content is, such as a museum photo or a swimwear product shot.', 'If other flagged posts exist, remove the ones you cannot defend.', 'Wait for the answer instead of resubmitting.']))],
 [('Can an automated flag be reversed?', 'Yes. Reviews sometimes restore content and accounts that were flagged by mistake. The decision is Instagram’s.'),
  ('Should I delete the flagged post?', 'Deleting borderline posts can help avoid more strikes, but keep a screenshot of the notice first.'),
  ('My business sells swimwear. Is that a problem?', 'Swimwear is allowed. Product shots that look too revealing can still be flagged, so describe the content clearly in the appeal.')],
 ['Photographers, artists and models.', 'Swimwear, fitness and lingerie brands.', 'Health and parenting creators.'])

page('instagram-suspended-for-bullying-or-harassment', F, 'Instagram Suspended for Bullying or Harassment',
 'Instagram suspended your account for bullying or harassment? How to read the notice, what is reviewable and how to write an appeal that stays factual.',
 'Instagram suspended you for bullying or harassment?', 'Suspended for bullying',
 'Heated comments, jokes taken out of context and reported replies can all end in a suspension. This is what to do.',
 'A bullying or harassment suspension is based on specific comments, captions or messages. Open the notice to see which one was flagged, then decide honestly whether it crossed the line. If it was a joke or context was missing, appeal and explain that factually, without attacking whoever reported it.',
 [('Read the flagged text', 'The notice points to the comment or message. Reread it as a stranger would.'),
  ('Add the context', 'If it was between friends or a reply to abuse, say so in one sentence.'),
  ('Stay calm in the appeal', 'Blaming the reporter weakens it. Stick to what was said and why.')],
 [S('What gets flagged', ul(['Insults or slurs, even when meant as jokes.', 'Repeated replies aimed at one person.', 'Posts that name or mock a private individual.', 'Group pile-ons where your comment was one of many.']),
    'Reports from several people at once make a flag more likely, whatever the intent.'),
  S('Writing the appeal', ol(['State what the flagged comment or message was.', 'Give the context in one or two sentences.', 'Say what you will do differently, if the content was out of line.', 'Submit once and keep the screenshot.']),
    'Do not message the person who reported you. It can lead to further reports.')],
 [('Can I appeal if I really wrote it?', 'You can, and honesty helps. Acknowledging that something was out of line reads better than denying it.'),
  ('What if I was reported falsely?', 'The appeal is still the route. Explain the context. Retaliating with reports of your own will not help.'),
  ('Does one comment cause a suspension?', 'It can, but accounts with earlier warnings are treated more strictly. Check Account Status for earlier flags.')],
 ['Creators with active comment sections.', 'Business owners reported by a dissatisfied customer.', 'People in heated group conversations.'])

page('instagram-suspended-for-selling-regulated-goods', F, 'Instagram Suspended for Selling Restricted Goods',
 'Instagram suspended your shop or account for selling regulated goods such as vapes, supplements or alcohol? What is allowed and how to appeal.',
 'Instagram suspended you for selling restricted goods?', 'Suspended for restricted goods',
 'Some products are not allowed to be sold through Instagram at all. Others are allowed with limits. Check which yours is.',
 'Instagram restricts the sale of certain goods, such as drugs, weapons, vapes and some supplements, and suspends accounts that list them. If your product is legal and allowed with conditions, appeal with a clear description. If it is prohibited, an appeal will not succeed, and it is better to know that early.',
 [('Check the category', 'Prohibited goods cannot be listed. Restricted ones may be, with limits.'),
  ('Describe the product', 'A plain product description helps a reviewer see what it is.'),
  ('Remove risky listings', 'Take down anything in a prohibited category before you appeal.')],
 [S('Prohibited and restricted', ul(['Prohibited: illegal drugs, weapons, ammunition, and similar items.', 'Restricted: alcohol, tobacco and vape products, some supplements, and age-gated goods.', 'Commonly misflagged: herbal teas, protein powder, legal hemp cosmetics, kitchen knives.', 'Services that sound like prohibited goods but are not.']),
    'Rules vary by country. What is legal where you sell may still be limited on the platform.'),
  S('If your product is allowed', ol(['Open the notice and request a review.', 'Describe the product in plain terms, with the category and the country you sell in.', 'Remove other listings that look similar but are not allowed.', 'Avoid wording in captions that sounds like a prohibited product.']))],
 [('Can a legal product still lead to a suspension?', 'Yes. Automated checks flag keywords and images, and some legal products sit in restricted categories.'),
  ('What if my product is prohibited?', 'Then an appeal is unlikely to work. We would tell you that rather than take on an appeal that has no chance.'),
  ('Will changing my captions help?', 'Clear, accurate descriptions help future reviews. They do not undo a past flag on their own.')],
 ['Small shops selling supplements, cosmetics or tea.', 'Sellers whose legal products were misread.', 'Brands that need to know whether a product is allowed.'])

page('instagram-suspended-scam-or-fraud-flag', F, 'Instagram Suspended for Scam or Fraud Activity',
 'Instagram suspended your account for scam or fraud activity you did not carry out? Common reasons, how to check your account and how to appeal.',
 'Instagram suspended you for scam or fraud activity?', 'Suspended for fraud flag',
 'A fraud flag feels serious because it is. Here is how to check for the usual causes and appeal properly.',
 'Instagram flags scam activity when an account promotes get-rich offers, asks for payments in DMs, uses fake giveaways, or was hacked and used for fraud. Work out which applies. If you were hacked, secure the email first, then appeal and say plainly that you did not send those messages.',
 [('Check for a hack', 'Odd DMs, unfamiliar logins or a changed email suggest someone else used the account.'),
  ('Review your giveaways', 'Giveaways that ask people to pay or send details can be read as scams.'),
  ('Secure the email', 'Whoever controls the email controls the account. Fix that before you appeal.')],
 [S('Common triggers', ul(['A hacker used the account to message followers about investments or loans.', 'Giveaways or promotions that ask for money, personal data or a login.', 'Links to pages that Instagram has flagged as unsafe.', 'Many people reporting the account at once after a dispute over an order.']),
    'Customer disputes are a common cause for small shops, even when you did nothing wrong.'),
  S('How to respond', ol(['Check recent messages and posts for anything you did not send.', 'Change the email password and turn on two-factor authentication if you can still log in.', 'Appeal from the notice and state whether you were hacked or whether this was a customer dispute.', 'Keep screenshots of order records if customers reported you.']))],
 [('Is a fraud flag harder to appeal?', 'Often, yes. Clear evidence that someone else used the account, or that orders were genuine, helps.'),
  ('Should I tell my followers?', 'If the account was hacked, a warning from another channel helps them avoid the scam. Do not use the suspended account.'),
  ('Can Unbanz prove I was hacked?', 'No. We help you organize the details and write the appeal. The decision belongs to Instagram.')],
 ['People whose account was used by a hacker for scams.', 'Online sellers reported after an order dispute.', 'Creators who ran a giveaway that was misread.'])

page('instagram-suspended-appeal-countdown-running', F, 'Instagram Suspended With a Countdown: What to Do',
 'Instagram shows a countdown on your suspension notice? What the timer means, why you should not wait and how to appeal before it runs out.',
 'Instagram suspension with a countdown running?', 'Suspension countdown',
 'A countdown on the notice is the window to appeal. Here is how to use it well.',
 'The countdown on an Instagram suspension notice is usually the time left to request a review before the account is permanently disabled. Do not wait to see if it resolves itself. Open the notice, submit one clear appeal, and keep a screenshot that shows the countdown and the date.',
 [('Do not wait it out', 'The timer is the appeal window, not a waiting period that ends the suspension.'),
  ('Appeal once, clearly', 'Use the in-app request and keep it factual.'),
  ('Capture the notice', 'A screenshot of the timer and date helps if you need to explain later.')],
 [S('What the timer is for', 'When Instagram flags an account it often gives a limited window to ask for a review. If the window closes without a request, the option can disappear and the account may be disabled for good.',
    ul(['The notice is shown on login, in the app or on the web.', 'The window differs by notice and is shown on the screen, not set by us.', 'Once it runs out, the appeal button may be gone.'])),
  S('What to do right now', ol(['Log in and open the notice. Take a screenshot including the timer.', 'Tap the option to request a review and follow each step, including any ID or selfie request.', 'Write a short explanation in your own words if asked.', 'Make a note of the time you submitted.']),
    'If the button does not work or loops, contact us before the window closes so we can look at routes together.')],
 [('What if the timer runs out?', 'The account may be disabled and the appeal option may disappear. There can still be other routes, so ask before giving up.'),
  ('Can I appeal more than once?', 'Follow the steps once and wait. Repeated submissions do not speed anything up.'),
  ('Does Unbanz stop the timer?', 'No. Nobody can stop it. The most useful thing is to submit an appeal while the option is there.')],
 ['People who just saw a suspension notice.', 'Business owners worried about their client accounts.', 'Anyone whose appeal button is about to disappear.'])

page('instagram-suspended-after-login-from-new-country', F, 'Instagram Suspended After a Login From a New Country',
 'Instagram suspended your account after you travelled or used a VPN? Why location changes trigger checks and how to get through the verification.',
 'Instagram suspended after logging in from a new country?', 'Suspended after new-country login',
 'Travelling, a new SIM or a VPN can make a login look like a takeover. Here is how to get through it.',
 'Instagram can suspend or lock an account when a login suddenly comes from another country or an unusual network, because it looks like a takeover. Use your normal phone and network, complete the verification steps in order, and appeal from the notice if it asks for a review.',
 [('Use your usual phone', 'Logging in from the device you normally use helps the checks pass.'),
  ('Turn the VPN off', 'A VPN or shared network can look suspicious. Use your usual connection.'),
  ('Follow every step', 'Complete each verification step in order instead of skipping to the next.')],
 [S('Why location changes matter', 'Instagram compares each login with your usual devices, networks and countries. A jump to a new country, a data-center address or a different device at once can look like someone else.',
    ul(['Travel and roaming SIM cards.', 'VPNs and proxy services.', 'Logging in from a new phone and a new country together.', 'Sharing the account with a team in other locations.'])),
  S('What to do', ol(['Go back to your usual device and network, if you can.', 'Open the notice and complete the verification, including a code or video selfie if asked.', 'If a review is offered, request it and explain that you were travelling or changed phone.', 'Avoid logging in from several places while the review is open.']))],
 [('Will it clear on its own?', 'Sometimes a verification step is enough. If a suspension notice appears, request a review.'),
  ('I run the account with a team. What now?', 'Agree on one way to access it. Many logins from different countries is a common cause. Official business tools are the safer route.'),
  ('Can Unbanz log in from my location?', 'No. We never log in. We help you prepare the appeal and the details.')],
 ['Travellers and people who moved country.', 'Teams that share one login.', 'Anyone who used a VPN to log in.'])

page('instagram-suspended-after-username-change', F, 'Instagram Suspended After Changing Your Username',
 'Instagram suspended your account right after you changed your username or display name? Why this looks like impersonation and what to do.',
 'Instagram suspended after a username change?', 'Suspended after username change',
 'A new name that resembles a brand or person can trigger an impersonation check. Here is what to do.',
 'Instagram suspends accounts right after a username or name change when the new name looks like another person or brand, or when the change came right after a login from somewhere new. Revert the name if you can, then appeal and explain that the name is your own and why you changed it.',
 [('Is the name yours?', 'If it resembles a brand or a known person, expect an impersonation check.'),
  ('Revert if possible', 'Going back to your earlier handle can help once access is restored.'),
  ('Explain the change', 'A short reason, such as a rebrand or business name, is usually enough.')],
 [S('Why a rename triggers checks', ul(['The new handle or display name matches a trademark, brand or public figure.', 'The change followed a login from a new device or country.', 'A hacker changed the name and the system flagged it.', 'Several changes in a short stretch.']),
    'Names with extra characters, a brand word or the word official are reviewed more closely.'),
  S('What to do', ol(['Check whether the name could look like someone else’s.', 'If you can still reach the account, revert to the earlier username.', 'Appeal from the notice and say the name is yours, with the reason you changed it.', 'Add your real business name or ID if they ask for proof.']))],
 [('Can I get my old username back?', 'Sometimes, if it was not taken in the meantime. Ask us if you want to check.'),
  ('Does the name change cause the suspension on its own?', 'Not always. It is often combined with another signal such as a new login.'),
  ('What if a hacker changed the name?', 'Then it is a recovery job as well as an appeal. Tell us what you can still access.')],
 ['People who rebranded and were suspended soon after.', 'Business owners using a brand-style handle.', 'Accounts that a hacker renamed.'])

page('instagram-suspended-for-following-too-many-accounts', F, 'Instagram Suspended for Following Too Many Accounts',
 'Instagram suspended your account for following or liking too much? What rate limits look like, why they hit new accounts and how to appeal.',
 'Instagram suspended you for following too many accounts?', 'Suspended for follow limits',
 'Following and liking in bulk is one of the quickest ways to get flagged, especially on a newer account.',
 'Instagram limits how many accounts you can follow, like or message in a short stretch, and newer accounts have lower thresholds. Going past them can lead to action blocks and, if it continues, a suspension. Stop the bulk activity, keep it light for a while, then appeal with an honest account of what you did.',
 [('Stop bulk actions', 'Following, liking and commenting in big batches is the trigger.'),
  ('Newer accounts are stricter', 'A fresh account with a lot of activity stands out more.'),
  ('Appeal after you stop', 'Say you have stopped and what you will do differently.')],
 [S('What counts as too much', 'Instagram does not publish exact numbers, and they change by account age and history. What matters is the pattern.',
    ul(['Following dozens of accounts within a few minutes.', 'Liking hundreds of posts back to back.', 'Follow, then unfollow, repeated across a list.', 'Using a bot or a script to do any of these.'])),
  S('What to do', ol(['Stop all bulk activity from the account.', 'Remove any tool that automates follows or likes.', 'Request a review from the notice and describe what you were doing honestly.', 'Keep activity gentle and human for a while after access returns.']))],
 [('Is following people against the rules?', 'No. Doing it in bulk, or with a tool, is what causes trouble.'),
  ('How long should I wait before using the account again?', 'There is no fixed time. Start gently once access is back and avoid bursts.'),
  ('Can Unbanz raise my limits?', 'No. Nobody can. We can only help you appeal a suspension.')],
 ['New accounts that grew by following others.', 'Social media managers doing outreach by hand.', 'Anyone who tried a growth tool.'])
